rustc_codegen_js: let `alloc` supply the default allocator.

`#![default_lib_allocator]` is how a crate tells rustc "the `__rust_alloc`
family is provided for you". `std` is the crate that normally carries it, and
without one rustc rejects every program that allocates and has no
`#[global_allocator]` of its own.

There is no `std` here. The `__rust_*` symbols are supplied by the backend
instead (`backend/src/alloc_support.rs`), with bodies over the runtime shim's
heap, and that is exactly what `AllocatorKind::Default` means. Marking `alloc`
as the crate providing it is what makes an ordinary `#![no_std]` crate with
`extern crate alloc;` compile with no allocator boilerplate at all.

--- a/library/alloc/src/lib.rs
+++ b/library/alloc/src/lib.rs
@@ -73,6 +73,10 @@
 #![feature(rustdoc_internals)]
 #![no_std]
 #![needs_allocator]
+// rustc_codegen_js: the `__rust_alloc` family is synthesized by the backend over the runtime
+// shim's heap, which is what `AllocatorKind::Default` asks for. Saying so here is what lets a
+// `#![no_std]` crate use `alloc` without a `#[global_allocator]` of its own.
+#![default_lib_allocator]
 // Lints:
 #![deny(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]
 #![deny(implicit_provenance_casts)]
